      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107559,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 266,
        "headingId": 470,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T11:54:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 708,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 471,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107560,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 266,
        "headingId": 471,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ovary</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T11:54:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 709,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 472,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107561,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 266,
        "headingId": 472,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>SCTAT can be seen at any age and may represent an incidental finding in Peutz&ndash;Jeghers syndrome (PJS). Non-syndromic cases may be associated with nonspecific symptoms, but estrogenic effects, seen in approximately 50% of patients, may cause isosexual pseudoprecocity or menstrual disturbance. Less commonly, there may be signs related to progesterone production {11493995; 5429475; 7104978; 25886261}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T11:54:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 710,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 473,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107562,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 266,
        "headingId": 473,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>SCTAT is rare overall, accounting for 1&ndash;2% of all sex cord tumours {7104978; 25886261}. In one large series, 36% of patients with sex cord tumours had PJS {7104978}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T11:54:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 711,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 474,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107563,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 266,
        "headingId": 474,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Approximately one&nbsp;third of SCTATs occur in patients with PJS, and about 75% of these patients have a germline mutation in <em>STK11</em>. The mechanism by which patients with PJS develop SCTAT is unclear {27241107}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T11:54:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 712,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 475,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107564,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 266,
        "headingId": 475,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Syndromic cases have germline <em>STK11</em> mutations on chromosome 19p13.3 {27821076}. SCTAT is not associated with <em>DICER1</em> mutations {33566167}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T11:54:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 719,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 482,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107565,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 266,
        "headingId": 482,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lesions in PJS are often small (microscopic to 30&nbsp;mm), bilateral, and multifocal. Non-syndromic tumours are typically unilateral tan to yellow masses as large as 200&nbsp;mm; they are usually solid, but cysts may be seen and occasionally predominate {5429475; 7104978}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T11:54:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 713,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 476,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107566,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 266,
        "headingId": 476,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Syndromic and non-syndromic tumours are both characterized by variably sized, usually rounded nests composed of simple or complex tubules that encircle hyaline basement membrane&ndash;like material, which may also be present around the tubules. Cells are tall, with pale cytoplasm and basally located round nuclei that often display an antipodal distribution within the tubules. In PJS lesions, calcification within the tubules may be present and there may be a solid proliferation of indifferent sex cord cells, whereas in non-syndromic cases classic morphology may focally transition to granulosa or Sertoli cell morphology. Cytological atypia and mitotic activity may be seen, rarely, in the non-syndromic setting {5429475; 7104978}. The tumour cells are typically positive for calretinin, WT1, inhibin, SF1, FOXL2, and CD56 {12966351; 17581419; 21378549; 12808064}. They are typically negative for EMA and CD10 {17885484}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T11:54:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 714,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 477,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107567,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 266,
        "headingId": 477,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T11:54:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 715,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
